when it comes to the villains of the  handmaid's tale aunt lydia is among the  worst charged with training and  disciplining handmaids lydia has enraged  and disturbed fans since the show's  first season this is her story from her  time as a teacher to her future in  gilead  in season three we're introduced to a  pre-gilead lydia clements played by anne  dowd as it turns out aunt lydia was once  an elementary school teacher with a  background in family law this lydia wore  her hair down and was once married even  in this era however lydia was pretty  judgmental especially when it came to  religion and parenting this is explored  in flashbacks which see lydia befriend a  young single mother named noel and her  son ryan the trio grow close and even  spend christmas together lydia seems to  revel in being called aunt lydia by ryan  however she frowns on what she sees as  some poor parenting choices by noel  speaking to vanity fair after the  episode dowd said i think she at that  point in her life really wants to help i  think she probably got out of family law  because it was too painful to see the  separation to see the loss the only  other time lydia alluded to her past was  during season 2's smart power after june  asks her to protect her unborn baby  i was godmother to my sister's child  he died when he was four days old  soon after noelle gives lydia a makeup  set for christmas lydia goes on a date  with jim the principal of the school  where she works but after they begin  kissing jim suddenly wants to stop while  he likes her jim thinks they're rushing  things instead of understanding lydia  flies into a rage then catches a glimpse  of herself with makeup running down her  face lydia's emotions here are complex  as ann dowd explained to vanity fair i  was raised in a strong catholic home you  know sex is a very very tricky area and  there's a lot of guilt and a lot of  shame associated with it i think what i  loved about that moment is it said so  much about how she was raised and that  honestly shame is in charge of her life  lydia's shame quickly turns to revenge  after blaming noel for convincing her to  go on the date she does everything in  her power to separate noel from her son  after noel angrily confronts lydia jim  walks often disgust after seeing lydia's  true colors she justifies her actions by  telling herself that ryan is better off  without noel  aunt lydia uses fear abuse and religious  manipulation to train handmaids to give  up their bodily autonomy for the sake of  gilead according to lydia the handmaid's  horrific lives are divinely ordained and  thus beyond human doubt flashbacks to  the red center see lydia training one of  the first groups of handmaids including  june moira and janine she is immediately  set up as a villain who uses torture to  get the handmaids to comply for example  she doesn't think twice about removing  janine's eye when janine resists  becoming a handmaid after the handmaids  are placed in homes lydia also serves as  a liaison between the government of  gilead and individual households she  frequently makes house calls to ensure  that everyone is following the rules in  the first season we see her make a  handful of visits to the waterford  residence where june has been placed  lydia frequently asserts her power over  june and at one point even beats her  with a cattle prod before the  commander's wife serena joy stops her  lydia is responsible for devising many  of gilead's abusive training techniques  which include ordering handmaids to hold  rocks for hours in the rain getting them  to kill traitors with their bare hands  and making them believe they're about to  be hanged she also orchestrates most of  the physical mutilation that occurs  including removing janine's eye and  performing female circumcision on emily  when she isn't violently disciplining  handmaids lydia is the ultimate  gaslighter she twists women  psychologically in a variety of ways  including victim blaming and setting the  handmaids against each other lydia is  particularly abusive towards june and is  quick to shame her into believing that  her choices are what bring harm and  death to others it's all done in a bid  to keep june compliant which works but  only for a while  lydia refers to the handmaids as her  girls in fact lydia often paints herself  as the handmaid's protector and is quick  to blame herself when they fail in her  own twisted way she believes she really  does care about them as dowd explained  in 2017 at the core of her choices she  loves these girls deeply and wants them  to succeed in this new world and she is  keenly aware that if she doesn't get  them to get the drill immediately they  are not going to make it this twisted  motherliness is apparent when serena  tells aunt lydia to remove janine from  an important dinner because of her  missing eye then lydia seems to  sympathize with jeanine but later she  proves her loyalty to gilead by ordering  the other handmaids to stone janine to  death the handmaids led by june refuse  rather than let the guards shoot them  for their disobedience lydia asks them  to spare the handmaids these girls are  my responsibility in season two it is  revealed that lydia sent janine to the  colonies and punished the handmaids with  a mock execution  emily suffers greatly at lydia's hands  after discovering that emily is in a  romantic relationship with a martha  lydia has emily's lover murdered and  emily mutilated when emily seeks revenge  by killing a guardian lydia has her sent  to the colonies she eventually brings  emily back because they're short on  handmaids and pairs her with commander  lawrence finally in the season 2 finale  emily stabs lydia in the back while  lydia survives the event changes her  making her an even harder character show  creator bruce miller explained the  impact emily had on lydia telling forbes  she thinks there's love between her and  her girls the fact that one of her girls  has literally stabbed her in the back i  think that alters your workplace  feelings on a day-to-day basis i think  in her case it makes her double down she  feels like she just wasn't strong enough  in her discipline so she has decided  it's time to get tough after emily's  escape from gilead at the end of season  two lydia can't take her feelings out on  her instead lydia comes down even harder  on the remaining handmaids eventually  she snaps after janine begs to return to  her former family and her infant  daughter lydia beats her mercilessly in  front of a crowd only stopping when june  comes between them  the relationship between lydia and june  is rough from the start after an  antagonistic first season they seem to  grow closer in season two as a pregnant  june leans into lydia seeking protection  for her unborn baby during this time  period we learn that lydia has some  power over the commanders and their  wives as well since she is tasked with  evaluating whether the household is a  good fit for a child the short-lived  peace between june and lydia doesn't  last however when june orchestrates her  daughter nicole's escape lydia comes  down hard on her she forces june to  participate in propaganda videos with  waterfords and even accompanies the  family on a trip to washington while  there she seems disturbed by the sight  of handmaids whose mouths have been sewn  shut lydia is very much aware that jun's  biggest weakness is her daughter hannah  who was kidnapped by gilead when june's  family tried to escape in season three  after june tracks down hannah lydia  orders the execution of the martha who  helped june connect with her daughter as  if that wasn't enough lydia forces june  and the other handmaids to be the ones  to execute her  after june helps a plane load of gilead  children flee to canada in the season 3  finale lydia comes under fire as a  result lydia makes it her mission in  season 4 to find and reform june  according to miller lydia in this season  is in a position where she's just  obsessed with june and has so much of  her personal worth tied up in what june  is doing as the season progresses lydia  and june continue to circle one another  although lydia never manages to keep  june within her clutches for long but  even when they're apart they each  continue to occupy a significant portion  of the other's thoughts miller explained  although june and lydia are kind of  enemies in this story they are  inextricably linked they spend a ton of  time thinking about the other one and  what the other one is doing so it's  fascinating to have two people who  really would like to be both rid of each  other but who are tangled up with each  other all the time  after june escapes yet again in season 4  aunt lydia is no longer in gilead's good  graces for the first time we see lydia's  sparse living quarters after being  stripped of her responsibilities she's  expected to spend the rest of her days  doing puzzles and playing cards with her  fellow aunts however lydia wants her  power back and is not interested in  being forced into an early retirement so  lydia strikes a deal with commander  lawrence she'll give him dirt on his  fellow commanders if he'll restore her  to her former station she also wants to  be the one to punish june if and when  she is finally caught  after lydia regains her power it's  evident that she is still haunted by her  failure with june after learning that  june escaped to canada she quickly  spirals out of control in episode 8 she  takes out her frustrations on one of her  charges and then even turns on a fellow  aunt speaking to elle dowd said she  holds herself responsible so do the  commanders she knew something was  happening she knew it and she did not  follow it to its core and june whom she  has a tremendously complicated  relationship with pulled it off so  there's that personal factor where she's  being tough on herself because she knows  all of that and also desperate to prove  that no no no i'm not irrelevant  after losing june lydia gets a second  chance to reform janine after janine is  recaptured in chicago following lydia's  outburst commander lawrence tells her to  take her anger out on janine whose life  is now considered expendable in a rare  moment of weakness lydia reunites with  janine and actually heeds her request  not to be assigned to another commander  instead lydia decides to keep janine at  the center at first the other ants  question why lydia isn't doing god's  work and putting janine back into the  field but lydia finds a use for her  before long when the former mrs keys  reappears as the handmade esther the  traumatized girl mounts a hunger strike  that none of the ants can stop aunt  lydia agrees to let janine talk to her  and janine is able to convince esther to  comply warning her that if she doesn't  the consequences will be worse than  death janine's assistance earns aunt  lydia's trust and helps re-establish  lydia's power among the other ants  while lydia's future within the world of  hulu's the handmaid's tale remains  unwritten margaret atwood took matters  into her own hands with her 2019 novel  the testament a sequel to her original  novel the testaments is written from  three perspectives including aunt  lydia's it takes place 15 years after  the events of the first novel which were  covered in the first season of the show  in this sequel atwood reveals that in  the early days of gilead lydia was  rounded up with many other women she was  detained tortured and eventually given a  choice cooperate or face the  consequences lydia sees herself as  someone who did what she had to do in  order to survive eventually in the book  she becomes integral to the fall of  gilead how much this novel will affect  the future of the show remains to be  seen atwood's lydia is a very different  character in many respects in the book  lydia was a judge prior to gilead and  acts as a mole for the rebellious  organization mayday for now one thing is  sure aunt lydia will definitely go down  as one of the most compelling tv  villains of all time check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  stuff for coming soon subscribe to our  youtube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

